  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1129 PM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nDry continental polar air will continue to modify over the\nMidsouth several days, as an upper level ridge builds in from TX.\nThis ridge will amplify to 586dam at 500mb by Tuesday, quite\nimpressive for mid/late November. The dry air and weak winds will\nresult in strong nocturnal cooling through midweek. Surface winds\nand dewpoints should recover sufficiently to preclude freezing\ntemperatures over all the Midsouth by Thursday.\n\nThe upper ridge will deamplify slightly as it shifts to the Ohio\nRiver Valley late this week. The net effect for the Midsouth will\nbe a return of west/southwesterly flow aloft. Medium range\nguidance consensus depicts an upper level trough passing through\nthe central plains late Saturday. A Pacific cold front will\naccompany this feature, but it may stall under a transition to\nzonal flow during the day Sunday.  LREF mean PWAT values approach\n1 inch over the Midsouth on Sunday, up from 0.3 inch earlier this\nweek. This would be our only chance of measurable rainfall, but\nit appears to be a modest chance, given the deamplified flow.",
